---
title: My C++ frontend idea
date: 2026-08-20
---
Owning my tools, for me, starts with the language. I've been playing with the idea of a language frontend for C++ for a
while now.

> Ironically, that's how C++ was born!

I envision a front-end for C++ which enforces the newer "style" of programming in the language we've seen become more
and more popular since C++11.

- Raw pointers are illegal, or are implicitly shared.

- The array operators use `std::array` under the hood.

- No header files.

And much more...

### Standing on some pretty relevant shoulders

This isn't exactly a new idea, which is comforting in a "I'm not crazy" kind of way.

Bjarne's original `cfront` did more or less this in reverse - it translated "C with Classes" into plain C, so you could
bootstrap an entirely new language on top of existing C compilers and linkers. Herb Sutter's
[cppfront](https://github.com/hsutter/cppfront) is the modern spiritual successor: a "syntax 2" for C++ that transpiles
down into today's syntax, opting you into memory-safety defaults, bounds-checking, and sane initialization rules without
needing a whole new compiler backend.

I'd be lying if I said I wasn't basically nodding along to cppfront's entire thesis. Where I differ is more about which
defaults I want flipped, and how opinionated the frontend should be about ownership.

I think the bulk of the work remains to be sketched out, and really as it stands, it's just an idea I'm playing around
with, but one I've been passionate about pursuing for a while now, so we'll see where it goes.